#### location ####
text: Tokyo Waterworks Historical Museum
----
lat: 35.7033379
----
lon: 139.7604921
#### text ####
text: After a stroll along the water, we had lunch at a nearby soba shop to get out of the freezing cold wind, then visited the Tokyo Waterworks Museum. It was fascinating learning how Tokyo managed fresh water and sewage in historical times. The old pipes were made of wood, and well sealed enough they could use the water pressure to go under roads. For areas that didn't have pipes yet, they would bring in the water - sometimes by boat! It seems very silly to have a boat (on the water) that's full of water, but it worked to carry fresh water over dirty water.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: Oidy5kTGcs_nZmzNnjzZyFGhRsU.jpg
-----
alt: The old wooden pipes. They're square and made of old wood, and have a section that goes down and then back up to go under a road.
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
##### image #####
image: EUeG8MFFP0uyWzuxGmwlmVXyd2U.jpg
-----
alt: Diagram showing a water network broken by a canal. A close up shows water going from a pipe into a boat on the canal.
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: They had a clever system so that fresh water and dirty water didn't mix but laundry could still be done around a communal well. The fresh water came in through underground pipes to the well, and a catchment system outside of the well collected the dirty water into surface-level drains. We had seen that architecture in historic areas on other trips but hadn't known why it was like that.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: rgfVxo1yWBq6fIIhLnTU4H9u1tE.jpg
-----
alt: Diorama of a village well, with a cutaway showing the fresh water coming to the well and the dirty water leaving via an different path
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
##### image #####
image: 5wy-WH1C2N9GdJgOJz-tTpea2n0.jpg
-----
alt: Recreation of an Edo-era square, showing the wooden well with buckets for washing, and the wooden slats over the surface drainage for dirty water.
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: Since Tokyo is on a floodplain, there was a lot about the various rivers, their watersheds, and how they were managed over the years.
#### image-collection ####
