#### text ####
text: I don't think I'll trust his medical advice though.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: fINQ5WT-0eB2WDbh3J1ghJ1nQvI.jpg
-----
alt: "The secret of a long life? His great interest in his self-made Chinese medicine. Hokusai suffered from a paralysis around 1827 when he was 68 but made a remarkable recovery with his self-made medicine. Itis prepared [by] chopping up a small citrus fruit called 'yuzu' with a bamboo spatula and boiling it down in an earthenware pot with 1.8 liters of ultra-fine sake into a syrup. Then diluted with hot water and divided into two doses to be taken within two days after the outbreak of an illness. He formulated other self-made medicines as well, writing at age 88 in a letter to a friend that people could stay completely healthy if they took two small cups, morning and evening, of a mixture of about 60 grams of skinned and dried longans (a fruit similar to a lychee), about 30 grams of white sugar, and 1.8 liters of ulta-fine shochu that had been enclosed in a pot and stored for two months.
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: At lunch, Dad went for the fanciest set with tempura and sashimi. I really enjoyed the tempura, which was more lightly battered than I'd had at home. I'd never had perilla leaf or fish as tempura before, and they're both really good!
