#### location ####
text: Osaka Castle
----
lat: 34.687256999999995
----
lon: 135.5258546
#### text ####
text: Next door and visible from the Osaka Museum of History is Osaka castle itself! It's in the middle of sprawling gardens and historic defensive structures, now a public park.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: hlC2vMeEcdaKRRhY4oHjaxilu24.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
##### image #####
image: gN17gi8WyMdfQ1frGw860K_wPOo.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: The current Osaka Castle is actually the third one. The first was white and it was burned in a battle at the beginning of the Tokugawa Shogunate. The second was black and built to be bigger and taller - even taller than the tallest shrines and temples! As punishment for the hubris, so the story goes, it was struck by lightning and burned down. The third and current one was built in the 1990s out of municipal pride (and as a tourist attraction) and blends elements of both previous castles, such as the colors.
#### text ####
text: James and I stayed outside and ended up having a lovely conversation in Japanese with some volunteer docents about the history of the castle, where we learned all that. It was a highlight of the trip and our language learning that we were able to make smalltalk and get through most of the history explanation in Japanese.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: FZe-aqKD8l-D5q62rK7DMt75ktY.jpg
