#### text ####
text: The next day we took a day trip into Kyoto to visit the other big train museum of the trip: the Kyoto Railway Museum!
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: 5Rd3qR44HY1XUEMI5M-yylDvtQQ.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
##### image #####
image: 6X0TFRhjLEo5e7ONVrklmGuyzJ8.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: On our previous trip, we had mostly looked at the rolling stock, so we spent more time with the exhibits this time. There's a lot of interesting info available!
#### text ####
text: One of the exhibits talked about the change in philosophy that accompanied the development of the Shinkansen - from 'how to stop trains' to 'how to run trains in a way that avoids collisions'. For example, ATC (automatic train control) automates knowing how fast the train should be going and slows it down as it approaches other trains, compared to the old style of having really good brakes.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: 2jDn_8-ckZ1mKsMOkiWu2KKHWrE.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: Like in Europe, trains motivated a change in how time was measured. Previously, the day had 6 hours whose lengths varied across the seasons and were marked by bells. With trains, the hours standardized on 24 hours of fixed length in a day.
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: Zf8bABozLxC1IlEKDAzL0pqz9N8.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: 
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: Foreign engineers, mostly from England, were paid a lot more than locals were, to the point that it was causing financial difficulties. Once a local engineering school was started, they started hiring mostly locals (which probably helped fix their budget).
#### image-collection ####
images:

##### image #####
image: rCOXMkPaZR1PNQg9Hbw-N0fkFyo.jpg
-----
alt: 
-----
css_classes: 
-----
caption: Japanese pay went from 40 yen to 350 yen, while foreigner pay went from 320 yen to 2000 yen
-----
video_link: 
#### text ####
text: At the time, everyone was excited about trains, including ladies who could have fans with a map of the train routes.
#### image-collection ####
